      </ret>
      <see>ex_login</see>
    </method>

    <method name="ex_SetProxy" qi="Allows optional specification of proxy service settings.">
      <p name="proxyURL" type="string" />
      <p name="proxyPort" type="string" />
      <p name="proxyUsername" type="string" />
      <p name="proxyPassword" type="string" />
      <ap>
        proxyURL : URL or IP address of proxy service.<br/>
        proxyPort : The proxy service connection port.<br/>
        proxyUsername : Username for the proxy server.<br/>
        proxyPassword : Password for the proxy server.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>

        The password can be encrypted by storing it in the Advanced section of the Custom tab in the Application Manager.
        The URL and user name parameters can be stored in the General section of the Datacap Application Manager but they cannot be encrypted.
        To retrieve the password that is stored in the Advanced section,
        use a smart parameter for the action's password parameter similar to
        @APPVAR(values/adv/mailProxyPassword) where mailProxyPassword is the name entered for the value in the Application Manager.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Optionally sets the values to a proxy service that will be used to connect to the email server.
        This action does not confirm that the connection to the proxy service is successful. Connection to the
        proxy service will not occur until the ex_login action is called.<br/><br/>

        If this action is used, it must be called prior to the ex_login action.  If this action is not
        called, then no proxy service will be used.
        <e>
          <b>ex_SetProxy("https://proxyurl.com", "8080", "proxyusername", "@APPVAR(values/adv/mailProxyPassword)")</b><br/>
          ex_login("https://mailserver.com/ews/exchange.asmx", "username@domain.com", "password")<br/><br/>

          This example sets up the parameters to use a proxy and then performs the login.
          The parameter for the password is an example smart parameter that obtains the password
          from an advanced value in the application service.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level, typically batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> if the proxyPort is not numeric, otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>ex_login</see>
    </method>

    <method name="ex_SetProxy_FullURL" qi="Allows optional specification of proxy service settings with Full URL.">
      <p name="proxyFullURL" type="string" />
      <p name="proxyUsername" type="string" />
      <p name="proxyPassword" type="string" />
      <ap>
        proxyFullURL : Full URL of proxy service.<br/>
        proxyUsername : Username for the proxy server.<br/>
        proxyPassword : Password for the proxy server.<br/><br/>

        Smart parameters are supported for all parameters.<br/><br/>

        The password can be encrypted by storing it in the Advanced section of the Custom tab in the Application Manager.
        The URL and user name parameters can be stored in the General section of the Datacap Application Manager but they cannot be encrypted.
        To retrieve the password that is stored in the Advanced section,
        use a smart parameter for the action's password parameter similar to
        @APPVAR(values/adv/mailProxyPassword) where mailProxyPassword is the name entered for the value in the Application Manager.
      </ap>
      <h>
        Optionally sets the values to a proxy service that will be used to connect to the email server.
        This action does not confirm that the connection to the proxy service is successful. Connection to the
        proxy service will not occur until the ex_login action is called.<br/><br/>

        If this action is used, it must be called prior to the ex_login action.  If this action is not
        called, then no proxy service will be used.
        <e>
          <b>ex_SetProxy("https://proxyurl.com:8080/proxy.url", "proxyusername", "@APPVAR(values/adv/mailProxyPassword)")</b><br/>
          ex_login("https://mailserver.com/ews/exchange.asmx", "username@domain.com", "password")<br/><br/>

          This example sets up the parameters to use a proxy and then performs the login.
          The parameter for the password is an example smart parameter that obtains the password
          from an advanced value in the application service.
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Any level, typically batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        <b>False,</b> if proxy settings fails, otherwise <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
      <see>ex_login</see>
    </method>

    <method name="ex_SetTopFolder" qi="Sets the top folder where search would start">
      <p name="root" type="bool" qi="A boolean value that sets the top folder to root"/>
      <ap>
        <b>True:</b> Searches done/problem folder from the root of the mailbox.<br/>
        <b>False:</b> Searches done/problem folder from the Inbox folder.<br/>
      </ap>
      <h>
        If this action is never called, ex_done_folder and ex_problem_folder search starts from the Inbox folder. (Default)
        If this action is called with parameter True, ex_done_folder and ex_problem_folder search starts from the root of the mailbox.
        <e>
          <b>ex_SetTopFolder("true")</b><br/>
          ex_done_folder("Imported")<br/>
          ex_problem_folder("Failed")
        </e>
      </h>
      <lvl>Batch level.</lvl>
      <ret>
        Always <b>True.</b>
      </ret>
    </method>

  </com>
</rrx>